# Segredos. O .env tem chaves de API, tokens do Discord, palavras-passe de
# Tuya/MIIO/Ewelink/Chacon e o token do bot. Nao deve nunca ser versionado.
.env
.env.*
*.env
!config-example.py
# Project lifecycle dirs. These WERE tracked (8 files under aes/) and the
# patterns below were already here -- .gitignore does not untrack, so the
# removal needed a separate `git rm -r --cached` commit. Anchored to the root
# so a same-named file in a subdir is still seen.
/aes/
# .aes/ holds per-machine working state (tickets, kanban, verification), which
# stays ignored. Its hooks/ are NOT state: they are the executable quality
# gates, and the pre-commit output points at them ("use config.py instead"),
# so a rule that is ignored is a rule the team does not have. A secret-gate
# fix landed only in the working copy passed here silently: every clone, CI run
# and second machine kept the old, weaker gate with no trace in the history.
# See SD-phantasma-OPS-020.
 /.aes/
# Only the gate scripts, not the rest of .aes/ (config.mk, plugins/ and the
# per-machine state beside them stay local). The patterns are anchored to
# /.aes/ so the un-ignore cannot leak into a same-named dir elsewhere.
!/.aes/hooks/
!/.aes/hooks/*.sh
# Generated by the AES bootstrap, per machine, and regenerated on every setup.
.aes/config.mk
.aes/harness.env
.aes/plugins/
# Dados em runtime
data/
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
brain.db
memory.db
cache/
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/
.pytest_cache/

# Ambiente Python
venv/
.pyenv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/

# Ferramentas / editores
.cache/
.config/
.viminfo
.bash_history
*.bak
*.bak-*
assistant.py.bak-*

# Node (playwright esta em node_modules/)
node_modules/
.pv/

# Temporarios
tmp/
*.log
*.pid
.assistant.pid

# Incident debris that must never be deployed. A .corrupt-.bak file was found
# inside src/api/ in production: the deploy rsync propagates anything in src/,
# so anything untracked here ends up served. Backups belong in /tmp, not the tree.
*.corrupt*
*.orig
*.rej
*~
# .env.example is a template, not a secret: it must stay tracked.
!.env.example

# 9.3 GB of pulled model weights plus an ed25519 PRIVATE KEY. Committing this
# would push a secret and 9 GB of blobs to a remote. Found while preparing the
# first real publish: the tree was 68 files "ready" and this sat in it.
ollama-data/
*.gguf
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.pub

# Third-party Piper TTS voices, 62 MB each. Downloaded artefacts, not source;
# the project's own wakeword models (models/*.onnx) stay tracked.
models/tts/pt_PT-dii-high.onnx
models/tts/en_US-glados-medium.onnx

# build artefact: pip install -e regenerates it, never version it
pHantasma.egg-info/

# Third-party JS for the 3D graph (three.js, OrbitControls, mermaid). Same
# reasoning as the Piper voices above: downloaded artefacts, not source, and
# ~1.4 MB. The graph does not render without them -- /admin/brain sits on
# "a carregar o grafo..." forever on a fresh clone. They live in
# /opt/phantasma/public/ in production and nowhere else in the repo; see
# docs/ROADMAP.md for the missing install step.
public/vendor/

# Zigbee2MQTT runtime data: the generated `advanced.network_key` and
# `coordinator_backup.json`. Both live in a named volume, not the tree, but a
# bind mount would put a Zigbee network key where the next `git add -A` would
# stage it -- the same failure as a Tuya key in git.
/zigbee2mqtt/*
# ...except the external converters, which are source: a human wrote them, they
# are the only thing that makes an unsupported device work, and they do not
# exist anywhere else if they are not in the repo. `/zigbee2mqtt/converters/`
# is the seed that `scripts/setup_zigbee.sh` copies into the volume.
/zigbee2mqtt/converters/
